<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">
<!-- SECURITY BOUNCER: a club with exactly one guest and a very strict doorman. It refuses outside scripts, network calls, forms and base-tag tricks. 'unsafe-inline' stays because a single-file page must run its own inline script. Could go wrong: a server-sent header or script hash would be stricter, and a meta tag cannot stop clickjacking. -->
<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy" content="default-src 'none'; script-src 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src data: blob:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'">
<!-- PRIVACY: no-referrer means that when she taps the Instagram link, Instagram is not told which page she came from. Even true love deserves a little mystery. -->
<meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#12030a">
<meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
<meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content="Our Date">
<title>Will You Go On A Date With Me?</title>
<style>
/* ===== 1. DESIGN TOKENS =====
   The wardrobe: every colour, corner size and font lives here once, so a mood change is one edit, not forty.
   SIX COLOURS, each with one job: night (the page), wine (the cards), cream (the words), rose-gold (the accent), deep rose (the main answer buttons) and paper (the keepsake card only). Soft rose is a lighter rose for small accents on dark.
   Left out on purpose: purple, neon, pure white, floating colour clouds and glowing shadows. The hearts that drift upward are the only thing on the page that moves on its own.
   THREE CORNER SIZES: r1 (tiny tags), r2 (buttons, fields, chips) and r3 (cards). Nothing is a pill or a circle except the little dots.
   Could go wrong: a colour typed directly further down will not follow these names, so search for the hex code before you rebrand. */
:root{--night:#12030a;--wine:#2a0c17;--wine2:#38101f;--cream:#fde8e2;--gold:#f3a78f;--rose:#e2476f;--rose-deep:#a30f39;--paper:#f5e6d8;--ink:#3a0c1c;
--edge:rgba(243,167,143,.6);--line:rgba(243,167,143,.28);--glass:rgba(255,255,255,.08);
--r1:6px;--r2:10px;--r3:14px;
--serif:Georgia,"Times New Roman",serif;--sans:system-ui,-apple-system,"Segoe UI",Roboto,sans-serif}
*{box-sizing:border-box;margin:0}
html,body{min-height:100%}
body{font-family:var(--sans);color:var(--cream);background:var(--night);line-height:1.5;
padding:env(safe-area-inset-top) 0 env(safe-area-inset-bottom);overflow-x:hidden}
/* BACKDROP: one still wash, black cherry at the top deepening a little toward the bottom. Nothing drifts and no coloured circles float behind the cards. position:fixed keeps it still while the page scrolls; pointer-events:none means it never steals a tap. */
body::before{content:"";position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:0;pointer-events:none;background:linear-gradient(180deg,var(--night) 0%,#1f0810 100%)}
/* SHINE: used once, on the big question. The headline is filled with a slow moving gradient (background-clip:text), like candlelight on silk. Could go wrong: without background-clip support the text would be invisible, so forced-colours mode (end of this file) turns it back into plain text. */
.shine{background:linear-gradient(90deg,#fde8e2,#f3a78f,#e8658a,#fde8e2);background-size:300% 100%;-webkit-background-clip:text;background-clip:text;color:transparent;animation:shine 9s linear infinite}
@keyframes shine{to{background-position:300% 0}}
::selection{background:var(--rose);color:#fff}
#bg{position:fixed;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;z-index:0;pointer-events:none}
/* THE STAGE: one centred column. It starts at the TOP (it is not centred up and down), so the progress dashes sit in exactly the same place on every step and the screen never jumps when she taps Next. The top padding grows a little on tall screens. */
main{position:relative;z-index:1;width:100%;max-width:560px;margin:0 auto;padding:clamp(12px,2.6vh,34px) 16px 8px;display:flex;flex-direction:column}
h1,h2{font-family:var(--serif);font-weight:400;line-height:1.15;letter-spacing:-.01em;text-wrap:balance}
h1{font-size:clamp(1.75rem,7vw,2.5rem)}
h2{font-size:clamp(1.5rem,6vw,2rem);margin-bottom:6px}
p.sub{opacity:.78;margin:8px 0 14px;line-height:1.5}
p.sub.fine{margin:12px 0 0;font-size:.85rem}
/* ===== 2. CARDS: ONE SKELETON =====
   Every screen is the same kind of card: the same wine colour, the same hairline border, the same corners, the same padding and the same heading position. Only the content changes. The one deliberate exception is the keepsake (#s5): it is paper, because it is the one thing she keeps.
   Each card has two parts. .lede is the QUESTION side (heading and a line of help). .pane is the ANSWER side (fields, chips, buttons). On a phone they simply stack. From 900px wide they sit side by side, question left and answer right.
   Only the card with .on is shown. Could go wrong: a new step with no matching id will simply never appear. */
.card{display:none;padding:22px 20px;border-radius:var(--r3);background:var(--wine);border:1px solid var(--line);animation:rise .4s ease-out}
.card.on{display:block}
#s4{text-align:center}
#s5{background:var(--paper);color:var(--ink);border-color:rgba(58,12,28,.25)}
@keyframes rise{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(12px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}
@keyframes shake{20%,60%{transform:translateX(-8px)}40%,80%{transform:translateX(8px)}}
@keyframes beat{0%,100%{transform:scale(1)}50%{transform:scale(1.08)}}
/* The Yes button's pulse is a ring that grows and fades. It is not a glow: nothing is drawn behind or under the button. */
@keyframes pulseRing{0%{box-shadow:0 0 0 0 rgba(226,71,111,.55)}70%,100%{box-shadow:0 0 0 12px rgba(226,71,111,0)}}
/* SHAKE: a polite 'hmm, not yet' wiggle when a required answer is missing. Never an error siren, just a raised eyebrow. */
.shake{animation:shake .4s}
/* WIDE SCREENS (900px and up): the stage widens, and every card splits into two equal columns. A card keeps the same width on every step, so nothing jumps sideways between steps. The short steps centre their answer side beside the question. */
@media (min-width:900px){
  main{max-width:1000px;padding:clamp(24px,7vh,90px) 32px 8px}
  .card{padding:38px 44px}
  .pane>:first-child{margin-top:0}
  .lede>:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
  .card.on{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr);column-gap:56px;align-items:start}
  .lede,.pane{min-width:0}
  #s0 .pane,#s4 .pane,#s6 .pane{align-self:center}
  /* THE KEEPSAKE in two columns: the left column holds the payoff (the heading, the plan, the personal note and Start over); the right column holds everything she can DO (WhatsApp, save, Instagram). On a phone the order is simply the order in the HTML: payoff first, actions next, note and Start over last. */
  #s5.on{grid-template-rows:auto auto 1fr}
  #s5 .lede{grid-column:1;grid-row:1}
  #s5 .pane{grid-column:2;grid-row:1 / span 3}
  #s5 .promise{grid-column:1;grid-row:2;margin-top:8px}
  #s5>.row{grid-column:1;grid-row:3;align-self:end;justify-content:flex-start}
}
/* ===== 3. CONTROLS =====
   Inputs, chips and buttons. .sel marks the answer she chose. Focus rings stay visible because keyboard users have hearts too. */
.names{display:grid;gap:10px;margin:0 0 4px}
.fields{display:grid;gap:10px;margin-bottom:4px}
input,textarea{width:100%;padding:12px 14px;border-radius:var(--r2);border:1px solid var(--edge);background:var(--wine2);color:inherit;font:inherit;color-scheme:dark}
input::placeholder,textarea::placeholder{color:inherit;opacity:.55}
input+.chips{margin-top:14px}
/* INTENTION DROPDOWN (first page): a real <select>, so phones show their own easy picker and screen readers know exactly what it is.
   It wears the same clothes as the name box (same border, same corners, same fill), so the two feel like one family.
   Plain words: .pick is the frame around the question and the box. .field wraps only the box, so the little arrow can sit on top of it. The arrow is two borders on a tiny turned square: no image, no icon file, no SVG.
   STATES: dimmed text while nothing is chosen, a gold border once she chooses, a rose border if she tried to continue without choosing.
   Could go wrong: on very narrow phones the longest choice is cut with ... inside the closed box (the open list always shows every word). Keep font-size at 16px or more, because iPhones zoom the whole page when a smaller field is tapped. */
.pick{margin-top:6px}
.pick label{display:block;margin-bottom:8px;font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;font-size:1.02rem;line-height:1.4;opacity:.9}
.pick .field{position:relative}
.pick .field::after{content:"";position:absolute;right:18px;top:50%;width:8px;height:8px;margin-top:-7px;box-sizing:border-box;border-right:2px solid var(--gold);border-bottom:2px solid var(--gold);transform:rotate(45deg);pointer-events:none}
.pick select{display:block;width:100%;padding:12px 42px 12px 14px;border-radius:var(--r2);border:1px solid var(--edge);background:var(--wine2);color:inherit;font:inherit;text-align:start;cursor:pointer;-webkit-appearance:none;appearance:none;color-scheme:dark;white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;transition:border-color .2s,background-color .2s}
.pick select:required:invalid{color:rgba(253,232,226,.7)}
.pick select:required:valid{border-color:var(--gold)}
.pick select:focus{border-color:var(--gold);background:#431522}
.pick select[aria-invalid="true"]{border-color:#ff9bb0}
/* The open list gets a solid wine background, because on Windows and Linux the list ignores see-through fills and would show pale text on white. */
.pick select option{background:var(--wine);color:var(--cream)}
.pick select option:disabled{color:rgba(253,232,226,.6)}
:focus-visible{outline:3px solid var(--gold);outline-offset:2px}
#s5 :focus-visible{outline-color:var(--rose-deep)}
h1:focus,h2:focus{outline:none} /* headings get focus only so screen readers announce each step */
.chips{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px;margin-bottom:14px}
.chip{padding:10px 14px;border-radius:var(--r2);border:1px solid var(--edge);background:transparent;color:inherit;font:inherit;cursor:pointer;transition:background-color .2s,border-color .2s,color .2s}
/* SELECTED CHIP: she should know at a glance that her tap registered. Three cues work together, so it never depends on colour alone: (1) a solid rose-gold fill with dark text (the light-and-dark swap is easy to see even without colour), (2) a soft ring around it, (3) a tiny pop when it lands. The script also sets aria-pressed for screen readers.
   Plain words: the ring is drawn with box-shadow, which never changes the chip's size, so the other chips do not move when she taps one. The text weight stays the same for the same reason: bold text is wider and would nudge its neighbours.
   Could go wrong: in forced-colours mode the browser removes all colours, so a system-colour version sits at the very end of this file. */
.chip.sel{background:var(--gold);color:var(--night);border-color:var(--gold);box-shadow:0 0 0 3px rgba(243,167,143,.3);animation:chipPop .3s ease-out}
@keyframes chipPop{0%{transform:scale(.95)}60%{transform:scale(1.04)}100%{transform:none}}
/* A press that answers instantly: the chip shrinks a little the moment a finger lands, before the script even runs. */
.chip:active,.chip.sel:active{transform:scale(.96)}
/* BUTTONS: one solid fill, no gradient. The main button is rose-gold with dark text on the wine cards, and deep rose with cream text on the paper card, so it is easy to see on both. The ghost button is an outline. Hover only changes the colour. */
.btn{padding:13px 22px;border-radius:var(--r2);border:0;font:inherit;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;background:var(--gold);color:var(--night);transition:background-color .2s,transform .15s}
.btn:active{transform:scale(.97)}
.btn.ghost{background:transparent;color:inherit;border:1px solid var(--edge);font-weight:400}
@media (hover:hover){.btn:hover{background:#f8baa5}.btn.ghost:hover{background:rgba(243,167,143,.12)}}
#s5 .btn{background:var(--rose-deep);color:#fdf1ea}
#s5 .btn.ghost{background:transparent;color:var(--ink);border-color:rgba(58,12,28,.45)}
@media (hover:hover){#s5 .btn:hover{background:#8a0c31}#s5 .btn.ghost:hover{background:rgba(58,12,28,.08)}}
#s0 .pane>.btn{width:100%}
.row{display:flex;gap:10px;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;margin-top:16px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.row.center{justify-content:center}
/* PROGRESS: five dashes, a 'Step 2 of 5' line and the whisper. They are the first things in the stage, so they never move. */
.dots{display:flex;gap:6px;justify-content:center;margin-bottom:6px}
.dots i{width:28px;height:4px;border-radius:2px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.2);transition:background-color .4s}.dots i.on{background:var(--gold)}
/* STEP LABEL: 'Step 2 of 5' sits right under the dashes. Small, quiet and centred, so it informs without shouting. Tabular numbers keep every digit the same width, so the line never wiggles when the step changes. min-height saves the space even when the line is empty (the goodbye screen), so nothing below it jumps.
   Could go wrong: the words are written by show(). If you add or remove a step, change the 5 there and the number of dashes in the HTML together. */
.stepnote{text-align:center;font-size:.78rem;letter-spacing:.03em;opacity:.78;min-height:1.2em;margin:0 0 6px;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
/* WHISPER: the italic line that changes with every choice. Two lines of room are always saved (three on very narrow phones), so the card below it never moves when the words change. */
.whisper{min-height:2.9em;text-align:center;font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--gold);margin:0 8px 12px;line-height:1.45;transition:opacity .25s}
@media (max-width:360px){.whisper{min-height:4.4em}}
/* ERROR LINES: gentle, in italics, with a line of room always saved so the page does not jump when one appears. */
.err{color:#ff9bb0;min-height:1.4em;margin:6px 0 4px;font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;font-size:.92rem;line-height:1.45}
/* ===== 4. THE BIG QUESTION =====
   One beating heart, two honest buttons. The No button is never hidden, moved or shrunk, because consent that has to chase a button is not consent. This is the one place the page spends its boldness: the shining headline, the beating heart and the pulsing ring on Yes. */
.ask{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px;align-items:center;margin:8px auto 4px;max-width:320px}
#yes{width:100%;font-size:1.1rem;background:var(--rose-deep);color:#fff4ee;animation:pulseRing 2.8s ease-out infinite}
@media (hover:hover){#yes:hover{background:#bd1646}}
#no{width:100%;background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--cream);font-weight:400}
.bigheart{background:none;border:0;cursor:pointer;display:block;margin:0 auto 2px;animation:beat 1.8s infinite;transition:filter .4s}
.bigheart.warm{filter:drop-shadow(0 0 18px rgba(243,167,143,.8))}
.bigheart:active{transform:scale(.92)}
.warmth{font-size:.8rem;opacity:.78;margin-bottom:12px}
.tease{min-height:4.8em;font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;line-height:1.5;margin:6px 4px 16px;transition:opacity .25s}
/* ===== 5. KEEPSAKE =====
   The plan card she screenshots. It lists the plan once, as a label-and-value list (a real <dl>), so the values stand out and a screen reader reads each pair together. The thank-you line is its signature. */
.ticket{margin:14px 0 16px;padding:14px 16px;border-radius:var(--r2);background:rgba(58,12,28,.07)}
.plan{display:grid;grid-template-columns:auto minmax(0,1fr);column-gap:14px;row-gap:6px;line-height:1.5}
.plan dt{opacity:.75}
/* A long word with no spaces (a pasted link, say) breaks inside the card instead of pushing the whole page sideways on a small phone. */
.plan dd{font-weight:600;overflow-wrap:anywhere;word-break:break-word}
.thanks{margin-top:12px;padding-top:12px;border-top:1px solid rgba(58,12,28,.2);font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;line-height:1.55}
/* The three save buttons share one row (two lines of text is fine when a label is long). */
.acts{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:8px;margin:12px 0}
.acts .btn{font-size:.82rem;padding:12px 4px;line-height:1.25;min-height:48px}
.textbtn{display:block;margin:2px auto 0;background:none;border:0;color:inherit;font:inherit;font-size:.9rem;text-decoration:underline;cursor:pointer;padding:12px;min-height:44px;opacity:.85}
.note{margin:4px 0 0;font-style:italic;text-align:center}
.note:empty{display:none}
/* ===== 5b. WHATSAPP PREVIEW (a small drawer under the WhatsApp box) =====
   The WhatsApp box above it is the shared .cta design (section 5c). This section styles the drawer: the exact message as a chat bubble, the 'someone is typing' moment, and the little hearts.
   WHY A DRAWER: the plan is already shown once, in the plan card. Showing it again inside the box would repeat it. So the exact words wait in a closed drawer ('See the message first'), and nothing is hidden or sent in secret: she can open it any time.
   HOW THE DATING-STYLE MOMENT WORKS, in plain words:
   1. At rest (no script, or her device asks for calm) the message is simply visible when the drawer is open. Nothing can go missing.
   2. The moment she taps the drawer open, the script adds .arm to the bubble. It shrinks to a small bubble with three bouncing dots, like someone typing, and the words wait out of sight. The script also saves the room the finished message will need (min-height on .chat), so nothing below it moves.
   3. A moment later the script adds .sent. The bubble pops open into the full message, the words rise in, and a few green hearts drift up from it. It plays once; opening the drawer again just shows the message.
   Could go wrong: the saved room is measured once, when the drawer opens. If the phone is turned sideways during the 1.4 second typing moment the message may differ in height by a few pixels; that is harmless and fixes itself when .sent clears the saved height. */
.cta-wrap{position:relative;margin:0 0 6px}
.cta-wrap>.cta{margin:0}
.cta-wrap>.fh{z-index:2}
.preview summary{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;min-height:44px;padding:8px 4px;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;font-size:.92rem;opacity:.9}
.preview summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none}
.preview summary::after{content:"";width:8px;height:8px;margin:-3px 4px 0 auto;box-sizing:border-box;border-right:2px solid currentColor;border-bottom:2px solid currentColor;transform:rotate(45deg);transition:transform .2s}
.preview[open] summary::after{transform:rotate(-135deg);margin-top:3px}
/* The chat area is a column pushed to the bottom, so the small 'typing' bubble sits at the bottom of the panel and the finished message grows upward from it, like a real chat. padding-right leaves room for the little tail. The panel is WhatsApp's dark green and the bubble is WhatsApp's own light green, with near-black text. */
.chat{display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:flex-end;align-items:flex-end;padding:14px 18px 14px 12px;border-radius:var(--r2);background:#0b3d2b}
.bub{position:relative;max-width:100%;padding:12px 14px;border-radius:16px 16px 4px 16px;background:#d9fdd3;color:#0b1f16;font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.5;text-align:left}
.bub::after{content:"";position:absolute;right:-8px;bottom:0;width:10px;height:14px;background:#d9fdd3;clip-path:polygon(0 0,100% 100%,0 100%)}
.bub .msg div{overflow-wrap:anywhere;word-break:break-word}
.bub .msg .gap{margin-top:.7em}
.bub .typing{display:none;gap:5px;padding:5px 2px}
.typing i{width:7px;height:7px;border-radius:50%;background:#1f6b45;animation:typeDot 1s infinite ease-in-out}
.typing i:nth-child(2){animation-delay:.15s}.typing i:nth-child(3){animation-delay:.3s}
@keyframes typeDot{0%,60%,100%{transform:translateY(0);opacity:.35}30%{transform:translateY(-5px);opacity:1}}
/* Typing: only the dots take up space. The words are lifted out of the flow (position:absolute) and hidden, but they stay in the page, so a screen reader can still read them. */
.bub.arm:not(.sent) .typing{display:flex}
.bub.arm:not(.sent) .msg{position:absolute;left:14px;right:14px;top:12px;opacity:0;pointer-events:none}
/* Sent: the bubble pops open from its bottom-right corner (where the tail is) and the words fade up inside it. */
.bub.arm.sent{transform-origin:100% 100%;animation:waPop .45s cubic-bezier(.2,.9,.3,1.25) both}
.bub.arm.sent .msg{animation:waIn .6s cubic-bezier(.2,.9,.3,1.2) .05s both}
@keyframes waPop{from{transform:scale(.88)}to{transform:none}}
@keyframes waIn{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(8px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}
/* FLOATING HEARTS: made by the script, moved by CSS, deleted when they finish. They are WhatsApp green, to match the box. --dx is how far each one drifts sideways. */
.fh{position:absolute;pointer-events:none;color:#3ee57c;line-height:1;opacity:0;animation:floatUp 1.7s ease-out both}
@keyframes floatUp{0%{opacity:0;transform:translate(0,0) scale(.6)}15%{opacity:.95}100%{opacity:0;transform:translate(var(--dx,0px),-90px) scale(1.15)}}
/* CALM MODE: no typing dots, the words are simply there, and no hearts. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.bub.arm:not(.sent) .typing{display:none}.bub.arm:not(.sent) .msg{position:static;opacity:1;pointer-events:auto}.fh{display:none}}
/* ===== 6. FOOTER, LEGAL LINKS, DIALOGS, TOAST =====
   The signature, the Privacy and Terms links, the transparent-code panel and the little confirmation bubble. Could go wrong: a fixed overlay on a tiny phone can hide content, so the footer and the legal links flow with the page. */
/* LEGAL LINKS: always on screen, on every step, because she should be able to read how her answers are treated BEFORE she types them. They are real buttons (not links), so nothing leaves the page. */
.legal{position:relative;z-index:1;display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center;gap:0 6px;margin:0 auto 14px;font-size:.78rem}
.link{background:none;border:0;color:var(--gold);cursor:pointer;font:inherit;text-decoration:underline;padding:8px 10px;min-height:32px}
/* FOOTER: flows at the end of the page (an earlier fixed version sat on top of the buttons). A quiet panel with a hairline border: nothing glows, nothing breathes, nothing lifts. */
footer{position:relative;z-index:5;margin:6px auto 24px;width:min(94%,520px);text-align:center;padding:10px 12px;font-size:.74rem;line-height:1.6;border-radius:var(--r2);background:rgba(255,255,255,.05);border:1px solid var(--line)}
/* CREDITS (audit button + signature footer): hidden while she is still answering, shown only on the two finish screens. The script puts .done on <body> inside show(). display:none (not just see-through) so nobody can Tab to a button they cannot see. When it appears it fades up gently; in calm mode the global rule switches the animation off and the block simply appears. */
.credits{display:none}
body.done .credits{display:block;animation:creditsIn .9s ease both}
@keyframes creditsIn{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(14px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}
/* SOURCE VIEWER: a full-screen room showing the code. Transparency is attractive. */
#src{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:20;background:rgba(12,2,8,.96);display:none;padding:14px;flex-direction:column;gap:10px}
#src.on{display:flex}
#src textarea{flex:1;font:12px/1.4 ui-monospace,Consolas,monospace;white-space:pre;color:#cfe}
.stats{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(110px,1fr));gap:8px}
.stats div{background:var(--glass);border-radius:var(--r2);padding:8px;text-align:center;font-size:.72rem;cursor:help}
.stats b{display:block;font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--gold)}
/* PRIVACY AND TERMS DIALOG: one small dialog, two short documents, a pair of tabs to switch between them. The words are ordinary text already in the page, so nothing is loaded and nothing is sent. The text area can take focus (tabindex 0 in the HTML), so a keyboard user can scroll it with the arrow keys. */
#legal{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:35;background:rgba(12,2,8,.94);display:none;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:14px}
#legal.on{display:flex}
.legalbox{display:flex;flex-direction:column;width:min(100%,640px);max-height:100%;padding:18px;border-radius:var(--r3);background:var(--wine);border:1px solid var(--line)}
.legalhead{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px}
.legalhead h3{font-family:var(--serif);font-weight:400;font-size:1.4rem}
.legaltabs{display:flex;gap:8px;margin:12px 0}
.legaltabs button{flex:1;padding:10px 8px;border-radius:var(--r2);border:1px solid var(--edge);background:transparent;color:inherit;font:inherit;cursor:pointer}
.legaltabs button[aria-pressed="true"]{background:var(--gold);color:var(--night);border-color:var(--gold);font-weight:600}
.legalbody{overflow:auto;padding-right:6px;line-height:1.55;font-size:.92rem}
.legalbody h4{font-family:var(--serif);font-weight:400;font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--gold);margin:16px 0 4px}
.legalbody h4:first-of-type{margin-top:8px}
.legalbody .stamp{font-size:.8rem;opacity:.75}
#toast{position:fixed;top:14px;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);background:var(--gold);color:var(--night);padding:10px 18px;border-radius:var(--r2);z-index:50;opacity:0;transition:opacity .3s;pointer-events:none}
#toast.on{opacity:1}
/* THE PERSONAL NOTE ('From me, honestly'): plain serif italics on the paper, set off by space and a small heading. No stripe and no box, because the words are enough. */
.promise{margin:20px 0 0;text-align:left}
.promise h3{font-family:var(--serif);font-weight:400;font-size:1.15rem;margin-bottom:6px}
.promise p{font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;line-height:1.65;font-size:.98rem}
.promise p+p{margin-top:8px}
/* ===== 5c. CALL-TO-ACTION BOXES: the WhatsApp message box and the Instagram follow box =====
   Two boxes, ONE design. How a box is built lives in the shared .cta rules: the grid, the padding, the corners, the 1px border, the dip on press, the tap ripple, and the way the first row (logo on the left, pill on the right) sits above a full-width heading. Each box only adds its own colours, in two small blocks: .cta-ig (Instagram: wine and rose-gold) and .cta-wa (WhatsApp: dark green). Change a shared rule and BOTH boxes change together, so they can never drift apart.
   The WHOLE box is one link, so a tap anywhere in it opens the other app (in a new tab, so her keepsake stays open).
   THE LOGOS are drawn with CSS only: no image, no icon file, no SVG. Instagram: a gradient tile, a camera outline, a lens ring and a flash dot. WhatsApp: a green tile, a speech bubble (a circle with one square corner) and a phone handset (a curved bar with a blob at each end). Every size is a fraction of --s, so a logo keeps its proportions at any size.
   WHAT MOVES, AND WHEN, in plain words:
   - Every 4.6 seconds a logo acts: it hops and tilts and a soft shine slides across it. Instagram's flash dot also lights up like a camera; WhatsApp sends out one green ping ring, like a new-message alert. The rest of the time everything sits still.
   - Nothing glows and nothing lifts on hover. Only two things answer her finger: the box dips when pressed, and a soft ripple spreads from the touch point (the script draws it).
   Could go wrong: motion can bother some people, so a phone set to 'remove animations' or 'reduce motion' gets still logos and no ripple. Instagram's gradient and WhatsApp's green are the bright colours here on purpose: they are what make the logos recognisable. */
/* THE TWO PALETTES. Each box sets the same named colours; the shared rules below simply use them. */
.cta-ig{--bg:#4a1024;--box-edge:var(--gold);--box-edge-hi:#ffd0bd;--box-ink:var(--cream);--box-ink2:var(--gold);--ripple:rgba(255,255,255,.3)}
.cta-wa{--bg:#0b3d2b;--box-edge:rgba(37,211,102,.75);--box-edge-hi:#86f5ae;--box-ink:#eafff1;--box-ink2:#9ff3bd;--ripple:rgba(130,255,180,.28)}
/* THE SHARED BOX. */
.cta{--s:48px;position:relative;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto;column-gap:14px;row-gap:10px;align-items:center;text-align:left;text-decoration:none;color:var(--box-ink);margin:0 0 12px;padding:14px;border-radius:var(--r3);background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--box-edge);-webkit-tap-highlight-color:transparent;touch-action:manipulation;transition:border-color .25s,transform .15s}
.cta>*{position:relative;z-index:1}
.cta>.cta-ripples{position:absolute;inset:0;z-index:0;border-radius:inherit;overflow:hidden;pointer-events:none}
.cta-logo{grid-column:1;grid-row:1;justify-self:start;display:block;width:var(--s);height:var(--s);transition:transform .2s}
.cta-head{grid-column:1/-1;display:block;font-size:clamp(.92rem,4.3vw,1.05rem);line-height:1.3;color:var(--box-ink);overflow-wrap:anywhere}
.cta-desc{grid-column:1/-1;display:block}
.cta em{display:block;font-family:var(--serif);line-height:1.5;font-size:.93rem}
.cta small{display:block;margin-top:6px;opacity:.9;font-size:.84rem}
.cta .igline{grid-column:1/-1;font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;color:var(--box-ink2);opacity:1;min-height:2.9em;margin-top:0;transition:opacity .35s}
.ighandle{color:#fff}
/* The pill: a plain solid button shape on the right of the first row. It is decoration for the finger (the whole box is the link), so it has no glow and no breathing. */
.cta-pill{grid-column:2;grid-row:1;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;text-align:center;line-height:1.15;min-width:92px;min-height:44px;padding:10px 18px;border-radius:var(--r2);font-weight:700;font-size:.98rem;transition:background-color .25s}
.cta-ig .cta-pill{background:var(--cream);color:var(--rose-deep)}
.cta-wa .cta-pill{background:#25d366;color:#04150c}
/* THE LOGO TILES. Both share size, rounded corners and the shine; each adds its own colours. overflow:hidden keeps the shine inside the corners. */
.iglogo,.walogo{position:relative;display:block;width:var(--s);height:var(--s);border-radius:calc(var(--s)*.28);overflow:hidden;forced-color-adjust:none}
.iglogo{background:radial-gradient(circle at 30% 107%,#fdf497 0%,#fdf497 5%,#fd5949 45%,#d6249f 60%,#285aeb 90%);animation:ctaNudge 4.6s ease-in-out infinite}
.walogo{background:linear-gradient(165deg,#63e57d 0%,#25d366 50%,#1da851 100%);animation:ctaNudge 4.6s ease-in-out infinite,waPing 4.6s ease-in-out infinite}
.iglogo::after,.walogo::after{content:"";position:absolute;inset:0;pointer-events:none;background:linear-gradient(115deg,transparent 35%,rgba(255,255,255,.38) 50%,transparent 65%);transform:translateX(-120%);animation:ctaSheen 4.6s ease-in-out infinite}
/* Instagram's white parts. */
.iglogo::before{content:"";position:absolute;box-sizing:border-box;inset:calc(var(--s)*.2);border:calc(var(--s)*.058) solid #fff;border-radius:calc(var(--s)*.17)}
.iglens{position:absolute;box-sizing:border-box;inset:0;margin:auto;width:calc(var(--s)*.28);height:calc(var(--s)*.28);border:calc(var(--s)*.058) solid #fff;border-radius:50%}
.igflash{position:absolute;box-sizing:border-box;left:calc(var(--s)*.6);top:calc(var(--s)*.31);width:calc(var(--s)*.085);height:calc(var(--s)*.085);border-radius:50%;background:#fff;animation:igFlash 4.6s ease-in-out infinite}
/* WhatsApp's white parts: the bubble is a circle whose bottom-left corner is left square; the handset is a quarter-ring with a small rounded block at each end. */
.walogo::before{content:"";position:absolute;box-sizing:border-box;left:calc(var(--s)*.2);top:calc(var(--s)*.18);width:calc(var(--s)*.6);height:calc(var(--s)*.6);border:calc(var(--s)*.06) solid #fff;border-radius:50% 50% 50% 0}
.waphone{position:absolute;box-sizing:border-box;left:calc(var(--s)*.37);top:calc(var(--s)*.34);width:calc(var(--s)*.26);height:calc(var(--s)*.26);border-left:calc(var(--s)*.07) solid #fff;border-bottom:calc(var(--s)*.07) solid #fff;border-radius:0 0 0 100%}
.waphone::before,.waphone::after{content:"";position:absolute;background:#fff;border-radius:calc(var(--s)*.03)}
.waphone::before{left:calc(var(--s)*-.07);top:calc(var(--s)*-.02);width:calc(var(--s)*.12);height:calc(var(--s)*.08)}
.waphone::after{right:calc(var(--s)*-.02);bottom:calc(var(--s)*-.07);width:calc(var(--s)*.08);height:calc(var(--s)*.12)}
@keyframes ctaNudge{0%,66%{transform:none}72%{transform:translateY(-7px) rotate(-5deg)}78%{transform:translateY(0) rotate(2deg)}84%{transform:translateY(-3px) rotate(-1deg)}90%,100%{transform:none}}
@keyframes ctaSheen{0%,70%{transform:translateX(-120%)}90%,100%{transform:translateX(120%)}}
@keyframes igFlash{0%,68%{transform:scale(1);box-shadow:0 0 0 0 rgba(255,255,255,0)}74%{transform:scale(2);box-shadow:0 0 8px 3px rgba(255,255,255,.9)}84%,100%{transform:scale(1);box-shadow:0 0 0 0 rgba(255,255,255,0)}}
/* The tap ripple: a soft circle that grows from the touch point and fades. The script sizes and places it. */
.cta-ripple{position:absolute;border-radius:50%;background:radial-gradient(circle,var(--ripple),rgba(255,255,255,0) 65%);transform:scale(0);animation:ctaRipple .7s ease-out forwards;pointer-events:none}
@keyframes ctaRipple{to{transform:scale(1);opacity:0}}
/* Screen-reader-only text: invisible, but still read aloud. */
.vh{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;margin:-1px;padding:0;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);clip-path:inset(50%);white-space:nowrap;border:0}
/* ===== 5d. SCROLL REVEAL =====
   Blocks marked data-rv in the HTML glide up a little (and most also fade in) the first time they scroll into view. 'slide' blocks only move, never fade, so they can never be invisible.
   SAFETY, in plain words: nothing is hidden until the script has switched on the class 'rv' on the page, and it only does that after the browser's scroll-watcher has reported back once, which proves the watcher works. If the watcher is missing or broken, or her device asks for calm, nothing is ever hidden, so there can never be a blank hole where a block should be.
   The script adds .in when a block comes into view, and .rv-quiet when the block was already on screen as its step opened (those just appear with the step and do not animate twice). --d is a tiny delay so blocks that arrive together glide in one after another. translate (not transform) is used so it never fights the hover and press effects. */
.rv [data-rv]:not(.in){opacity:0}
.rv [data-rv="slide"]:not(.in){opacity:1;translate:0 22px}
.rv [data-rv].in{animation:rvIn .75s cubic-bezier(.2,.7,.2,1) var(--d,0s) both}
.rv [data-rv="slide"].in{animation-name:rvSlide}
.rv [data-rv].in.rv-quiet{animation:none}
@keyframes rvIn{from{opacity:0;translate:0 20px}to{opacity:1;translate:0 0}}
@keyframes rvSlide{from{translate:0 22px}to{translate:0 0}}
/* AUDIT BUTTON AND REPORT PANEL: the part of the page that asks 'are we okay?' and answers honestly instead of saying 'we're fine'.
   Severity is written as a coloured tag with the WORD in it (red critical, orange high, amber medium, green low, blue informational), so colour never works alone. There is no coloured stripe down the side of a finding. */
/* KEY GATE: a small centred dialog that guards the source viewer and the audit report. */
#gate{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:40;background:rgba(12,2,8,.9);display:none;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:18px}
#gate.on{display:flex}
.gatebox{width:min(100%,380px);padding:22px;border-radius:var(--r3);background:var(--wine);border:1px solid var(--edge)}
.gatebox h3{font-family:var(--serif);font-weight:400;font-size:1.4rem;margin-bottom:6px}
.auditbtn{display:block;margin:12px auto 0;padding:12px 20px;border-radius:var(--r2);background:rgba(255,255,255,.08);color:var(--cream);border:1px solid var(--edge);font:inherit;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;position:relative;z-index:5;transition:background-color .2s,color .2s}
.auditbtn:hover{background:var(--gold);color:var(--night)}
#audit{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:25;background:rgba(12,2,8,.97);display:none;flex-direction:column;padding:14px;gap:10px}
#audit.on{display:flex}
#auditBody{flex:1;overflow:auto;padding-right:4px}
#auditBody h3{font-family:var(--serif);font-weight:400;color:var(--gold);margin:18px 0 4px}
.fi{margin:8px 0;padding:10px 12px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.06);border-radius:var(--r2);font-size:.88rem;line-height:1.45}
.fi p{margin-top:4px;opacity:.85}
.badge{display:inline-block;font-size:.7rem;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:var(--r1);background:rgba(255,255,255,.16);margin-right:6px}
.badge.st-passed{background:#1f6b45}.badge.st-detected{background:#8a2a2a}.badge.st-fixed{background:#2a5a9a}.badge.st-notperformed{background:#6a5a1a}
.badge.sv-critical{background:#b3123f;color:#fff}.badge.sv-high{background:#a8440f;color:#fff}.badge.sv-medium{background:#7a5b00;color:#fff}.badge.sv-low{background:#1f6b45;color:#fff}.badge.sv-informational{background:#2a5a9a;color:#fff}
/* LOGO KEYFRAMES. Instagram's flash dot lights like a camera. WhatsApp's logo sends out ONE green ping ring at the moment of the hop (the ring is a box-shadow that grows and fades, like a new-message alert). Neither one glows the rest of the time. */
@keyframes waPing{0%,66%{box-shadow:0 0 0 0 rgba(37,211,102,0)}72%{box-shadow:0 0 0 0 rgba(37,211,102,.6)}92%,100%{box-shadow:0 0 0 14px rgba(37,211,102,0)}}
/* Mouse only (so a phone does not leave a box 'stuck' after a tap): the border brightens and the pill changes colour. The press rules come LAST so a press always wins over a hover. */
@media (hover:hover){
  .cta:hover{border-color:var(--box-edge-hi)}
  .cta-ig:hover .cta-pill{background:#ffd9cc}
  .cta-wa:hover .cta-pill{background:#52ec8d}
}
.cta:active{transform:scale(.985)}
.cta:active .cta-logo{transform:scale(.92)}
/* Bigger on roomy screens. The heading sits BELOW the logo row, so it always gets the full width of the box. */
@media (min-width:480px){.cta{--s:54px;padding:16px 18px;column-gap:16px}}
/* REDUCED MOTION: if her device asks for calm, every animation and transition is switched off. Romance should not make anyone dizzy.
   Plain words: the star (*) means 'every element', but it does NOT include the invisible extra pieces a page draws with ::before and ::after (the shine on the logos is drawn that way). So those two are named too. Without them a phone set to 'remove animations' would still show the shine sweeping across. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){*,*::before,*::after{animation:none!important;transition:none!important}}
/* ===== 7. THE HONEST LINES (first page) =====
   Five short sentences that arrive one at a time, like someone saying them out loud and letting each one land.
   HOW IT WORKS, in plain words:
   1. At rest (no script, or her device asks for calm) every sentence is simply visible and every underline is fully drawn. Nothing can go missing.
   2. The script "arms" the block by adding .vals-live. Now each sentence starts hidden (see-through, a little low, a little blurry) and each underline starts at length zero.
   3. When the block is on screen the script adds .go. Each sentence then fades in on its own timer (--d). The three key phrases follow, each with its own gesture:
      honesty is written left to right, face to face closes in from both ends, real emotions opens outward from the middle.
   4. When the last underline finishes, the script removes both classes. That leaves the block in its resting look, identical to the last frame, so nothing jumps and nothing replays if she taps Back.
   TYPE: the sentences are italic serif, the same voice as the whisper line and the promise note. The key phrases stand up straight in roman, which is how typographers mark emphasis inside italic text.
   Could go wrong: the delays below are one timeline. If you reorder the sentences in the HTML, reorder the :nth-child rules too. The script ends the show when the underline on .hl-c finishes, so keep the valsDraw name on it. */
.vals{--vals-ink:rgba(253,232,226,.9);--vals-gold:var(--gold);--vals-rose:#ff9db5;margin:16px 0 14px;max-width:27em;display:grid;gap:.35rem}
.vline{font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;font-size:clamp(1rem,4.2vw,1.2rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--vals-ink);text-wrap:balance}
.vlead{font-style:normal;font-size:clamp(1.2rem,5.2vw,1.5rem);line-height:1.25;letter-spacing:.01em;color:var(--cream);white-space:nowrap;margin-bottom:.3rem}
/* THE THREE KEY PHRASES: honesty, face to face, real emotions. The underline is not a border. It is a thin strip painted as a background image along the bottom edge, which lets it grow smoothly. The anchor point decides where it grows from: the left edge (honesty), both edges (face to face: two strips a little over half the width, so they overlap in the middle) or the centre (real emotions). padding-bottom lowers the strip so it clears the tail of the letter y.
   Could go wrong: nowrap keeps each phrase on one line so its underline never splits in two. That is fine while the phrases stay short. */
.hl{font-style:normal;font-weight:400;white-space:nowrap;padding-bottom:.1em;color:var(--vals-gold);background-repeat:no-repeat}
.hl-a{background-image:linear-gradient(var(--vals-gold),var(--vals-gold));background-position:0 100%;background-size:100% 2px}
.hl-b{background-image:linear-gradient(var(--vals-gold),var(--vals-gold)),linear-gradient(var(--vals-gold),var(--vals-gold));background-position:0 100%,100% 100%;background-size:51% 2px,51% 2px}
.hl-c{color:var(--vals-rose);background-image:linear-gradient(var(--vals-rose),var(--vals-rose));background-position:50% 100%;background-size:100% 2px}
/* GET READY (the script adds .vals-live): sentences start see-through, a little low and a little blurry; phrases start in the plain text colour with no underline. */
.vals-live .vline{opacity:0;transform:translateY(12px);filter:blur(6px)}
.vals-live .hl{color:inherit}
.vals-live .hl-a,.vals-live .hl-c{background-size:0% 2px}
.vals-live .hl-b{background-size:0% 2px,0% 2px}
/* THE TIMELINE (the script adds .go): when each sentence starts (--d), then when each key phrase gets its colour and underline, a moment after its sentence has settled. Total: about 8.6 seconds, and the form below works the whole time.
   Could go wrong: will-change is only set while the show runs, because keeping it forever wastes memory. */
.vline:nth-child(1){--d:.6s}
.vline:nth-child(2){--d:1.9s}
.vline:nth-child(3){--d:3.2s}
.vline:nth-child(4){--d:4.7s}
.vline:nth-child(5){--d:6.7s}
.vals-live.go .vline{animation:valsIn 1.2s cubic-bezier(.22,.8,.3,1) var(--d,0s) both;will-change:opacity,transform,filter}
.vals-live.go .hl-a{animation:valsGold .9s ease-out 3.9s both,valsDraw .9s cubic-bezier(.65,0,.35,1) 3.9s both}
.vals-live.go .hl-b{animation:valsGold .9s ease-out 5.6s both,valsDrawB .9s cubic-bezier(.65,0,.35,1) 5.6s both}
.vals-live.go .hl-c{animation:valsRose 1s ease-out 7.3s both,valsDraw 1.3s cubic-bezier(.65,0,.35,1) 7.3s both}
@keyframes valsIn{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(12px);filter:blur(6px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none;filter:blur(0)}}
@keyframes valsGold{to{color:var(--vals-gold)}}
@keyframes valsRose{to{color:var(--vals-rose)}}
@keyframes valsDraw{to{background-size:100% 2px}}
@keyframes valsDrawB{to{background-size:51% 2px,51% 2px}}
/* CALM MODE: the rule above already stops animations on elements, but it cannot show what the script had hidden. If her device asks for calm after the script armed the block, this puts every word and underline at rest at once. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
.vals-live .vline{opacity:1;transform:none;filter:none}
.vals-live .hl-a,.vals-live .hl-b{color:var(--vals-gold)}
.vals-live .hl-c{color:var(--vals-rose)}
.vals-live .hl-a,.vals-live .hl-c{background-size:100% 2px}
.vals-live .hl-b{background-size:51% 2px,51% 2px}
}
/* HIGH-CONTRAST MODE (Windows and others): the system repaints backgrounds, so the painted underline would vanish. A real text underline takes its place. */
@media (forced-colors:active){.hl{background:none;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:.2em}}
/* FORCED COLOURS: the system paints everything in its own palette and ignores glows, so the selected chip is shown with the system's own 'selected' colours and an outline, and the chat bubble gets a real border. */
@media (forced-colors:active){.chip.sel{background:Highlight;color:HighlightText;border-color:HighlightText;forced-color-adjust:none;outline:3px solid Highlight;outline-offset:2px}.bub{border:1px solid CanvasText}.bub::after{display:none}.cta-pill{border:2px solid ButtonText}}
/* SHORT PHONES (700px tall or less, like an iPhone SE): trim the space between things a little, so the first page keeps its Begin button comfortably on screen without scrolling. Taller phones keep the airier spacing. It sits after the honest-lines rules on purpose, so it can adjust them. */
@media (max-height:700px){
  main{padding-top:10px}
  .card{padding-top:16px;padding-bottom:16px}
  .whisper{margin-bottom:8px}
  p.sub{margin:6px 0 10px}
  .vals{margin:10px 0 10px;gap:.25rem}
  .vline{font-size:.98rem;line-height:1.4}
  .vlead{font-size:1.15rem}
  .err{margin:4px 0 2px}
}
/* MORE FORCED-COLOURS CARE: the shining headline is painted with a background, which the system removes, so it becomes plain text; the keepsake list and its panel get real borders. */
@media (forced-colors:active){.shine{background:none;color:CanvasText}.ticket{border:1px solid CanvasText}.plan dt{opacity:1}.card{border:1px solid CanvasText}}
</style>
