<body>
<!-- BACKGROUND CANVAS: floating hearts and confetti are painted here by hand (no libraries). aria-hidden because decoration should not be read aloud. Sits behind everything and ignores taps. -->
<canvas id="bg" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
<!-- TOAST: tiny bubble that says 'Copied' and vanishes. role=status lets screen readers announce it politely. -->
<div id="toast" role="status"></div>
<!-- MAIN STAGE: one column, one card visible at a time. The dashes, the step line and the whisper sit at the very top and never move. Every card has the same two parts: .lede (the question side) and .pane (the answer side). On a phone they stack; from 900px wide they sit side by side. -->
<main>
  <!-- PROGRESS: five dashes for the five steps (name and hope, mood, day, time and place, the big question). The keepsake screen is the finish line, so it is not a step: it lights every dash and says 'All set'. The dashes are decoration, so they are hidden from screen readers; the words under them are real text, so everyone can tell where they are. The first dash starts lit because the page opens on step 1. -->
  <div class="dots" id="dots" aria-hidden="true"><i class="on"></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
  <p class="stepnote" id="stepNote">Step 1 of 5</p>
  <!-- WHISPER: the italic line that changes with every choice she makes. aria-live=polite so screen readers can follow without interrupting. -->
  <p class="whisper" id="whisper" aria-live="polite"></p>

  <!-- STEP 0, THE INVITATION: collects her name and what she is hoping to find. Both are required, because we are charming, not anonymous. Could go wrong: spaces-only names are rejected by trimming in reqStep(), and the dropdown's first option is disabled, so the hint text can never be chosen as an answer. -->
  <section class="card on" id="s0">
    <div class="lede">
      <h1 tabindex="-1">Something wonderful is waiting for you</h1>
      <p class="sub">Three small choices, then one big question. About 30 seconds.</p>
      <!-- HONEST LINES: five short sentences that arrive one at a time (the looks are in CSS section 7, the timing in script section K). They are ordinary text, so a screen reader simply reads them in order, and if the script never runs they are just there, fully visible. The three key phrases are strong because they carry the most weight. Could go wrong: if you reorder these lines, reorder the nth-child delays in the CSS too. -->
      <div class="vals" id="vals">
        <p class="vline vlead">Self-Managed.</p>
        <p class="vline">If it matters, say it.</p>
        <p class="vline">I respect <strong class="hl hl-a">honesty</strong>.</p>
        <p class="vline">Some things are better <strong class="hl hl-b">face to face</strong>, not assumed.</p>
        <p class="vline"><strong class="hl hl-c">Real emotions</strong> matter more than interests.</p>
      </div>
